const samples={
answer:{title:'“Is this actually worth building?”',source:'A VERY FAMILIAR KIND OF ANSWER',question:'Could eye tracking make long AI answers easier to skim?',text:`There are several interesting ways to approach the relationship between attention and information on the internet. Most people already scan long responses for a few useful ideas before deciding what deserves a closer look. Build a reading lens that highlights the useful sentence in the paragraph you are looking at.

The temptation is to make the entire page smarter, with summaries, rewritten explanations, and a constantly changing layout. That can introduce more work for the reader because the words keep moving just as they start to make sense. Keep the original text still and change only its emphasis.

Eye tracking makes the interaction feel different because your attention becomes an input. A regular webcam can estimate a region of the screen after calibration. But it is not reliable enough to assume you know the exact word someone is reading. If calibration is poor, let the reader use their pointer instead.

There are several ways to decide which sentence should stand out. A local scoring system can look for actions, numbers, and qualifications without sending the text anywhere. Jev could choose among the original sentences when a user explicitly enables the cloud option. Neither method can prove that a highlighted claim is true.

The most useful next step is a small test with real reading tasks, rather than another long feature list. Ask five people to find an answer in the same passage, with and without the lens, and check whether they understood the caveat. If the lens feels distracting or slows them down, that is a reason to change the interaction.`},
article:{title:'A better relationship with the wall of text',source:'A SHORT ORIGINAL ESSAY',question:null,text:`The internet has become exceptionally good at producing text. Every question can become an essay, every meeting can become a transcript, and every notification can lead to another page. The scarce resource is the reader's attention.

We do not approach every paragraph with the same intention. Sometimes we want a decision, sometimes a number, and sometimes the one condition that changes everything. Useful emphasis should respond to what the reader is looking for.

A conventional summary gives you a new document to read. It can be useful, but it also separates a statement from its surrounding explanation. A reading lens can leave the original document in place and make one sentence easier to notice.

This comes with a responsibility. A highlighted number can look authoritative even when the underlying claim has no evidence. A qualification that begins with “unless” may matter more than the sentence that sounds most confident. Emphasis is a reading aid, not a credibility signal.

The goal is not to make people read every sentence faster. It is to help them choose where to slow down. Test whether readers answer questions more accurately and remember the limitations before claiming that a lens improves comprehension.`}
};
let sample='answer',custom=null,goal='answer',topic='',model=null,active=0,mode='pointer',paused=false,lens=true,pins=[],visits=[],candidate=-1,focusTimer=null,demoTimer=null,demoTick=0,cloudConfigured=false,cloudEnabled=false,requestGeneration=0,rankController=null,toastTimer=null,lastGazeAt=0,smoothPoint=null;
